THREADED INTERPRETIVE LANGUAGE ENVIRONMENT (TILE)November 30, 1989Mikael R.K. PatelComputer Aided Design Laboratory (CADLAB)Department of Computer and Information ScienceLinkoping UniversityS-581 83 LINKOPINGSWEDENEmail: mip@ida.liu.se1.     INTRODUCTIONTILE Forth is a Forth-83 implementation written in C. Thus allowing it tobe easily moved between different computers compared to traditional Forthimplementations in assembler. TILE Forth is organized in a set of modules to allow the kernel to be usedas a general threading engine for C. Also environment dependencies suchas memory allocation, error handling and input/output are separated toincrease flexibility. The kernel supports the Standard Forth-83 Word Set except for blockswhich are not used. The kernel is extended with the following; argumentbinding and local variables, queue management, low level compiler words,null terminated string functions, exceptions and multi-tasking. The TILE Forth environment also contains a set of source files for high level multi-tasking, data structuring and a number of programming tools.To allow interaction and incremental program development TILE Forthcontains a programming environment. This environment helps with program structuring, i.e., pretty printing, documentation search, andprogram development. Each vocabulary in the kernel and the source filesis described by a documentation and test file. This style of programmingis emphasized throughout the environment.So far TILE Forth has been ported and tested to over twenty locationswithout any major problems except where C compilers do not allow sub-routine pointers in data structures.Most Forth implementations are done in assembly to be able toutilize the underlying architecture as optimal as possible. TILE Forthgoes an other direction. The main idea behind TILE Forth is to achievea portable forth implementation for workstations and medium sizecomputer systems so that new groups of programmers may be exposed to the flavor of an extensible language such as Forth. The forthapplication is just an example of how to use the kernel. The organization of TILE Forth is selected so that, in principle, anyC-level procedure may become available on the interactive andincremental forth level. Other models of implementation of a threadedinterpreter in C are possible but are not as flexible.TILE Forth is divided into three major parts, the kernel, io-, error-, and memory management packages, and the application example, TILE Forth itself. TILE Forth extends the Forth 83 required standard with many of theconcepts from modern programming language such as argument binding, and local variable frame, multi-tasking, exception management etc.Writing a Forth in C gives some possibilities that normally arenot available when performing the same task in assembler. TILE Forthhas been profiled using the available tools in Unix. This informationhas been used to optimize the compiler so that it achieves a compilationspeed of over 100.000 lines per minute on a Sun-4.Currently code is only saved in source form and applications aretypically compile-and-go. Comparing with the traditional benchmark such as the classical sievescalculation is difficult because of difference in speed betweenworkstations and personal computers. The Byte sieves benchmark isreported to typically run in 16 seconds on a DTC forth implementation.This benchmark will run in 27 seconds in TILE forth on a SUN-3/60.This is the total time for starting forth, compiling and executing the benchmark.Comparing to, for instance, other interpretive languages such as Lisp, where one of the classical benchmarks is calculation of the Fibonacci function, the performance increase is almost two magnitudes.2.     EXTENSIONSWhat is new in the forth vocabulary? First of all is the overallorganization of words. Vocabularies, casting and entry caching isused to make word search faster, but also to make programs written in TILE Forth more portable to other forth dialects. If a vocabulary other than forth is used the target system much realize these. The casting operator in the forth top loop, interpret, allow selection of words from vocabularies without changing the search order thus operator overloading may be achieved. In general, the top loop recognizes a casting operation as a parenthesized vocabulary name prefix. An example; selecting string concatenation without changing the search order just write:       (string) +As Forth lack tools for description of data structures TILE Forth contains a fairly large library of tools. These are described more in detail in the next section.When writing a forth function with many arguments stack shufflingbecomes a real pain. Argument binding and local variables are niceways out of these situations. Also for the new-comer to Forththis gives some support. Even the stack function may be rewritten:       : 2swap { a b c d } c d a b  ;       : 2drop { a b } ;An other extension in TILE Forth are exception handing with multipleexception handling code block. The syntactical structure is veryclose to that of Ada, i.e., any colon definition may contain an errorhandling section. Should an error occur during the execution of thefunction the stack status is restore to the situation at calland the last exception block is executed. Error situations may beindicated user an exception raise function. Low level errors, such as zero division, are transformed to exceptions in TILE Forth.       : div ( x y -- z)          /       exception> ( x y exception -- )         true abort" div: zero division attempted"         ;Last some of the less significant extension are forward declarationof entries, hidden or private entries, and extra entry modes. Threenew entry modes have been added to the classical forth model (immediate).These allow hiding of entries in different situations.The first two marks the last defined words visibility according toan interpreter state. These two modifiers are called "compilation" and "execution" and are used as "immediate". A word like "if" is"compilation immediate" meaning it is visible when compiling and thenalways executed.        compiler forth definitions       : if ( -- ) compile (?branch) >mark ; compilation immediateThe "private" modifier is somewhat different. If concerns thevisibility across vocabularies. If a word is marked as "private" the word is only visible when the vocabulary in which it is definedin is "current". This means that if the vocabulary is not "current"and in the search chain "context" the word is not visible. This is very close to the concept of hidden in modules and packages in Modula-2 and Ada.       4 field +name ( entry -- name) privateThe above definition will only be visible in the vocabulary it was defined. This is useful to isolate implementation dependencies and reducethe name space.3.     SOURCE LIBRARYThe TILE Forth programming environment contains a number of tools to makeprogramming in Forth a bit easier. If you have GNU Emacs, TILE Forth may run in a specialized forth-mode. This mode supports automatic program indentation (pretty printing), documentation search, interactive andincremental program development. To aid program development there is also a source code library withdocumentation (glossary), and test and example code. Most of the sourcecode are data modeling tools. In principle, from bit field definition toobject oriented structures are available. The source code library alsocontains debugging tools for tracing, break-point'ing and profiling of programs. The first level of data modeling tools are three modules for describing;1) bit fields, 2) structures (records), and 3) aggregates of data (vectors,stacks, buffers, etc).The next level of tools are some tools for high level syntactic sugarfor multi-tasking concepts (semaphores, channels, etc), finite statemachines (FSM), and anonymous code block (blocks).The source library will be extended during the coming releases (seethe release plan, file: RELEASES).4.     PROGRAMMING STYLEA source code module has, in general, the following structure; First section includes any modules needed (theses are only loaded once).Second follows global definitions for the module. Normally this is a vocabulary for the module. Third comes the search chain to be usedthroughout the module. It is important not to change the search orderas 1) it becomes difficult for a reader to understand to code, 2)any change in the search chain flushes the internal lookup cachein TILE Forth.       .( Loading the Library...) cr       #include someLibrary.f83       ( Global data and definitions)       vocabulary theLibrary       someLibrary theLibrary definitions       ( Local data and definitions)       : someDefinitions ( -- ) ... ;        : somePrivateDefinitions ( -- ) ... ; private       forth onlyTo create lexical level within the same vocabulary the word "restore" maybe used. It stores the vocabulary pointer to the given entry and thushides the words defined after this entry. The word "restore" has much thesame action as "forget" but without putting back the dictionary pointer (dp).Each module should also be documented according to the template documentationfile. This format allows the documentation function to work properly.5.     SOURCE FILESThe TILE Forth source is broken down into the following files:tile   The TILE Forth source code and documentation library.tile.1   Manual for "man" under Unix.README   This short documentation of TILE.COPYING   The GNU General Public License.RELEASES   The current plan of source code releases.PORTING   Some help on how to port TILE Forth and typical problemsMakefile   Allows a number of compilation styles for debugging, profiling, sharing   etc. New machines and conditional compilation symbols are added here.forth.c   The main program using the multi-tasking kernel. Also gives access to   program arguments and start symbol.forth.el   Forth Mode for GNU Emacs. Allows interactive program development in   EMACS and documentation search.kernel.c..h   The multi-tasking Forth kernel. error.c..h   The signal and error handling package of TILE Forth.io.c..h   The input and output management package of TILE Forth.memory.c..h   Memory allocation package of TILE Forth.src   The Forth source library with data description, high level tasking and   other tools.tst   Test file for each Forth source code file and a set of benchmarks.doc   Documentation and glossary for each source code file and kernel   vocabularies.The documentation follow the C style and the glossary structure is usedby the Forth Mode search function.6.     CONFIGURATIONTILE forth is targeted for 32-bit machines and no special aid is availableto allow it to be compiled for other bit-widths. The configuration is maintained by a "make" files. The configuration file allows a number of different modes to supporttypical program development phases (on C level) such as debugging, profiling, optimization and packaging. Please see the information inthese files.7.     COPYINGThis software is offered as shareware. You may use it freely, but if you do use it and find it useful, you are encouraged to send theauthor a contribution for the next project (an object oriented threadedinterpreter -- `the flexibility of Smalltalk to the speed of Forth').For further information about copying see the file COPYING and theheader in each source code file.8.     NOTEDue to the 32-bit implementation in C a number of Forth definitions are not confirmed. Below is a short list of words that might give problemswhen porting Forth code to this environment:* The Block Word Set is not supported. Source code is saved as text files.* All stacks and words size are 32-bit. Thus special care must be taken  with memory allocation and access.* Lowercase and uppercase are distinguished, and all forth words are  lowercase. * A word in TILE is allowed arbitrary length as the name is stored as  as a null terminated string.* Input such as key performs a traditional read operation to the  operation system thus will echo the characters.* Variables should not allocate extra memory. "create" should be used.ACKNOWLEDGMENTSFirst of all I wish to express my gratitude to Goran Rydqvist for helpedme out with the first version of the kernel and who implemented the forth-mode for GNU Emacs. Second a special thanks to the beta test group who gave me valuablefeedback. Especially Mitch Bradley, Bob Giovannucci Jr., Moises Lejter, and Brooks David Smith. Thank you all.